---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import MergeAlternatePdfPages from '@/components/tools/pdf/MergeAlternatePdfPages.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="merge-alternate-pdf-pages">
  <MergeAlternatePdfPages client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>The duplex scan problem</h2>
      <p>
        Cheap sheet-feed scanners and most multifunction printers scan only one side at a time. For a
        double-sided document, you feed the stack and get a PDF of the fronts, then turn the stack
        over, feed it again and get a second PDF of the backs. Neither file is usable alone, and the
        second is in reverse order, because the turned stack puts the last sheet on top.
      </p>
      <p>
        To merge alternate PDF pages fixes it in one step: front 1, back 1, front 2, back 2, and so
        on, in one document with the pages in reading order.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Worked example</h2>
      <p>A ten-page, five-sheet document scanned in two passes:</p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Output page</th>
            <th scope="col">Comes from</th>
            <th scope="col">Which page of that file</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>File A (fronts)</td><td>1</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2</td><td>File B (backs, reversed)</td><td>5 (was scanned last)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3</td><td>File A</td><td>2</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4</td><td>File B, reversed</td><td>4</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5</td><td>File A</td><td>3</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6</td><td>File B, reversed</td><td>3</td></tr>
          <tr><td>…</td><td>…</td><td>…</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        The order preview above the button shows this mapping for your two files before the tool
        writes anything, so you see a wrong reverse setting at once, before you print 200 sheets.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>When to turn the reverse switch off</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Off if you rotated the stack and did not turn it over, or if your scanner software already
          reversed the second pass.
        </li>
        <li>
          Off if the two files come from different sources, for example alternate pages of a
          translation and its original.
        </li>
        <li>
          On (the default) for the usual case, where you turn the stack over.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If you are not sure, look at the preview. If output page 2 comes from the highest page number
        in file B, the reverse is on.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Uneven page counts</h2>
      <p>
        Most real documents have them, usually because the last sheet was printed on one side. With
        the default, <strong>keep the extra pages at the end</strong>, the extra pages are added at the
        end. Choose <strong>stop when the shorter file runs out</strong> when the backs are blank and
        you do not want them.
      </p>
      <p>
        A difference of more than one page usually means a misfeed: two sheets went through together on
        one pass. Scan again, and do not patch. The preview shows which pass is short.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Nothing is re-compressed</h2>
      <p>
        The tool copies pages between documents as complete PDF objects. The scan images keep their
        original resolution and compression, colour profiles stay, and an OCR text layer from your
        scanner stays complete and searchable. The output is the same page content in a new order,
        not a new render.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Related fixes for a bad scan</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/reverse-pdf-page-order">Reverse PDF Page Order</a> when only one file is in the wrong direction.</li>
        <li><a href="/split-pdf-pages-in-half">Split PDF Pages in Half</a> when two pages are on one scan.</li>
        <li><a href="/rotate-pdf">Rotate PDF</a> when the backs came out upside down.</li>
        <li><a href="/delete-pages-from-pdf">Delete Pages from PDF</a> for the blank backs of single-sided sheets.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
